ABSTRACT
When students or beginners want to break into a tech career — Web Development, Data Analytics, Android App Development, and so on — they routinely get lost. Every course starts from zero, even for skills the learner already has, and there is no clear answer to “what do I learn next, and how long will it take?” This wastes time and causes tutorial fatigue, where learners abandon long generic courses without ever reaching a job-ready skill set.
PathForge is a personalised career roadmap and skill diagnostic web platform built to solve exactly this problem. Instead of pushing every learner through the same long course, PathForge first benchmarks what the student already knows through an adaptive diagnostic quiz and an interactive skill-check matrix. It pre-credits the skills the student has already mastered, so they are not forced to relearn them, and then generates an ordered, milestone-by-milestone roadmap tailored to one of five in-demand career tracks: Full-Stack Web Development, Data Analyst, UI/UX Designer, Android App Developer, and Digital Marketing Specialist.
The roadmap can be explored through a Timeline/Network view, an IDE Workbench with project instructions and documentation links, and a drag-and-drop Kanban board (To Learn / In Progress / Mastered). A Sprint Planner with a pace slider (5 to 40 hours per week) projects an estimated graduation date, and a built-in Pomodoro focus timer supports study sessions. An Analytics Dashboard shows a donut progress ring, study hours completed versus remaining, and a daily learning streak counter, while a theme customiser offers dark/light mode and four accent colour themes.
The application is built with React 18, TypeScript and Tailwind CSS, bundled with Vite, and currently runs as a front-end prototype with all data (career tracks, milestones, quiz questions) stored locally in seedData.ts and browser localStorage. A companion PHP 8 and MySQL (PDO) reference architecture is included in-app for academic viva demonstration, alongside the modern cloud deployment on Vercel. This report describes the existing problem, the requirement specification, the system analysis and design using standard diagrams, the implementation with screens, the roadmap-generation algorithm and code, the test cases, the user manual, and the limitations and future enhancements of the project.
Keywords: career roadmap, skill diagnostic, adaptive quiz, React, TypeScript, Tailwind CSS, Vite, Progressive front-end prototype, Vercel.
