4.2 Program Specifications / Flow Charts
The Roadmap Engine follows a fixed algorithm: load every milestone for the chosen track, skip the ones the diagnostic already confirmed as mastered, then use the remaining milestones and the student’s pace to compute an estimated graduation date.
 
Figure 4.7 – Flow chart of the roadmap-generation algorithm
Algorithm summary
Step	Rule
1. Load milestones	Fetch every milestone belonging to the selected career track, in Fundamentals → Intermediate → Advanced order.
2. Pre-credit check	For each milestone’s required skill, check the DiagnosticResult; if it is confirmed Mastered, mark the milestone Mastered and exclude its hours from the remaining total.
3. Build roadmap	Every non-mastered milestone is kept, in order, as the student’s active roadmap.
4. Sum hours	Add the estimatedHours of every remaining milestone to get the total hours left.
5. Apply pace	Divide total hours by the pace slider value (hours per week) to get the number of weeks remaining.
6. Graduation date	Add that many weeks to today’s date to produce the estimated graduation date shown in the Sprint Planner.
4.3 Code Snippet (Roadmap Engine & PHP/MySQL Reference)
The core personalisation logic lives in one TypeScript function, generateRoadmap, which pre-credits mastered skills and returns only the milestones the student still needs.
Roadmap Engine (TypeScript)
export function generateRoadmap(
  track: CareerTrack,
  allMilestones: Milestone[],
  result: DiagnosticResult
): Milestone[] {
  const trackMilestones = allMilestones
    .filter(m => m.trackId === track.id)
    .sort((a, b) => STAGE_ORDER[a.stage] - STAGE_ORDER[b.stage]);
 
  return trackMilestones.map(m => {
    const mastered = result.masteredSkillIds.includes(
      m.relatedSkillId
    );
    return {
      ...m,
      status: mastered ? 'Mastered' : 'ToLearn',
    };
  });
}
 
export function estimateGraduationDate(
  roadmap: Milestone[],
  paceHoursPerWeek: number
): string {
  const remainingHours = roadmap
    .filter(m => m.status !== 'Mastered')
    .reduce((sum, m) => sum + m.estimatedHours, 0);
 
  const weeksLeft = Math.ceil(remainingHours / paceHoursPerWeek);
  const gradDate = new Date();
  gradDate.setDate(gradDate.getDate() + weeksLeft * 7);
  return gradDate.toISOString().split('T')[0];
}
Explanation
•	generateRoadmap filters the full milestone list down to just the selected track, sorts by stage, and marks each milestone Mastered or ToLearn by checking whether its skill appears in the student’s masteredSkillIds.
•	estimateGraduationDate only looks at milestones that are still ToLearn or InProgress, sums their estimated hours, and divides by the student’s weekly pace to get weeks remaining — this is exactly what re-runs live when the Pace Slider moves.
•	Both functions are pure — given the same inputs they always return the same output — which is what lets the UI recalculate instantly without a server round-trip.
