G. Likely viva questions and answers
Basics
Q1. What is your project?
A personalised career roadmap and skill diagnostic platform that benchmarks a student’s existing skills and generates a custom, milestone-driven learning path for one of five tech career tracks.
Q2. What problem does it solve?
Tutorial fatigue — generic courses force every learner to start from zero, wasting time on skills they already have and giving no honest estimate of how long the goal will take.
Q3. What are the 5 career tracks?
Full-Stack Web Development, Data Analyst, UI/UX Designer, Android App Developer, and Digital Marketing Specialist.
Q4. Who is the only user of this app?
The learner/student themselves — there is no separate admin role, since it’s a personal learning tool.
Technology
Q5. Why React 18?
Component-based architecture — one reusable component like <MilestoneCard /> is built once and reused across every milestone instead of duplicating code.
Q6. Why TypeScript?
Strongly typed data structures (Student, Milestone, DiagnosticResult) catch mistakes at compile time and make the roadmap logic safer to change.
Q7. Why Tailwind CSS?
Fast utility-first styling used to build the dark/light themes and the four accent colour options quickly and consistently.
Q8. Why Vite?
Very fast development server and a small, optimised production bundle.
The roadmap engine
Q9. How does pre-crediting work?
For every milestone, the engine checks whether its required skill is in the student’s masteredSkillIds list from the diagnostic; if yes, the milestone is marked Mastered and excluded from the remaining-hours calculation.
Q10. How is the graduation date calculated?
Sum the estimatedHours of every milestone that is not yet Mastered, divide by the student’s weekly pace, round up to whole weeks, and add that many weeks to today’s date.
Q11. Why is generateRoadmap a pure function?
So the same track, milestones and diagnostic result always produce the same roadmap — this makes it predictable, testable, and fast enough to rerun instantly in the browser.
Q12. What’s the difference between the adaptive quiz and the check matrix?
The quiz asks 5 real questions and scores the answers; the check matrix lets the student instead pick a preset (Fresh Beginner, CS Student, Career Switcher) that applies a predefined mastered-skill list directly, skipping the quiz.
Data and storage
Q13. Where is student progress stored?
In the browser’s localStorage — Kanban status, pace, theme and streak all persist there between visits on the same browser.
Q14. Where do the tracks and milestones come from?
A local mock data file, seedData.ts, rather than a live server — this is explicitly listed as a current limitation.
Q15. What happens if I open the app on my phone after using it on my laptop?
Progress does not carry over, because localStorage is per-browser/per-device; this is a known limitation, with a cloud database planned as a future enhancement.
Design and analysis
Q16. Explain your use cases.
8 use cases — select track, take diagnostic, view roadmap, manage Kanban, adjust pace, use Pomodoro timer, view analytics, customise theme — all performed by the single Student actor.
Q17. What is the difference between DFD Level 0 and Level 1?
Level 0 shows the whole system as a single process; Level 1 breaks it into four sub-processes (diagnostic, roadmap generation, progress tracking, analytics) with three data stores.
Q18. What does the sequence diagram show?
The order of messages from submitting the quiz, through roadmap generation, pace-slider recalculation, and a Kanban status update reaching localStorage.
Testing
Q19. How did you test the project?
Manual functional testing with 15 test cases covering track selection, the diagnostic, roadmap pre-crediting, Kanban drag-and-drop, the pace slider, the Pomodoro timer, analytics, theming, persistence and the production build.
Q20. Give one test about the pace slider.
Moving the pace from 10 to 20 hours/week on the same remaining-hours total should roughly halve the estimated weeks remaining.
Deployment
Q21. How did you deploy it?
Pushed to GitHub, imported the repo into Vercel with Framework: Vite, Build command: npm run build, Output directory: dist; also ships as a single frontend_standalone.html file.
Q22. Why does your project include both Vercel and XAMPP-style code?
The live app is a modern React SPA best served by Vercel’s global CDN; the PHP/MySQL reference code is kept in-app so I can demonstrate a traditional relational-database architecture on the college’s XAMPP setup during the viva, since that’s the classic BCA curriculum standard.
Limitations and future
Q23. What are the current limitations?
Progress is localStorage-only (no cloud sync), all content is mock data from seedData.ts, and there’s no social login or real user accounts yet.
Q24. What would you add next?
A cloud database (PostgreSQL or MongoDB) for real accounts, an AI-powered mentor using the Gemini API for code feedback, and a LinkedIn/resume PDF exporter.
